-Reporting: If you find a vulnerability, please open a Private Advisory on GitHub instead of a public issue.
+## 6. Code guidelines
 
-## 🤝 Community Governance
-Be respectful. We are building this together.
+- Use type hints for new functions.
+- Log with `orchestrator.utils.logging.get_logger`, not `print()`. CLI output goes through `rich`; escape
+  external text with `rich.markup.escape` before printing it.
+- Start subprocesses with argument lists, never with shell strings that contain user input. Quote values for
+  remote shell commands with `shlex.quote`.
+- External text (web pages, logs, probe files, knowledge base) reaches an LLM only through
+  `orchestrator/utils/untrusted.py`.
+- New actions for AI clients are tools in `orchestrator/tools/framework_tools.py` with the correct tier
+  (`read`, `local_write`, `external`). Never bypass the registry, the permissions or the audit log.
+- Do not weaken the build sandbox or the SSH trust rules.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Commits and pull requests
+
+- Branch from `main`; one topic per pull request.
+- Commit messages: a summary line in English, in the imperative, sentence case, no type prefix, about 72
+  characters. Example from the history:
+  `Harden SSH: trust on first use with fingerprints, framework key, no stored passwords`. The body explains
+  why.
+- Code, tests, documentation (English and German) and the changelog entry belong in the same pull request.
+- Describe in the pull request how you tested, for example which board and which model.
+- Never commit secrets or personal state: `configs/secrets.store`, `configs/config.yml`, API keys, tokens, or
+  files from `~/.llm-framework`.
+- No binaries: no `.whl`, `.so` or model files. Download them in the Dockerfile and check their hashes.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Changelog
+
+- Format: [Keep a Changelog 1.1.0](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/); versions follow
+  [Semantic Versioning 2.0.0](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).
+- Every change users notice gets an entry under `## [Unreleased]` in `CHANGELOG.md`, and the same entry in
+  German in `CHANGELOG_DE.md`. Use the sections Added, Changed, Deprecated, Removed, Fixed and Security, and the
+  matching sections in the German changelog.
+- Write for users: what changed and what it means for them.
+- Planned work belongs in the roadmap ([docs/BLUEPRINT.md](docs/BLUEPRINT.md#roadmap)), not in the changelog.
+- For a release, the maintainers turn `[Unreleased]` into `## [x.y.z] - YYYY-MM-DD` and set the same version in
+  `orchestrator/__init__.py` (`__version__`) and `pyproject.toml`.
+
+---
+
+## 9. Documentation
+
+- English is the primary language, German the second. **Every document exists twice:** `<name>.md` (English)
+  and `<name>_DE.md` (German), with the same structure and content.
+- Write natural German, not a word-by-word translation.
+- Both files link to each other at the top. Every document starts with a line like
+  `Version 3.0.0 · Last updated 2026-10-10` (German: `Version 3.0.0 · Stand 2026-10-10`); update it when you
+  change the document. README and changelog carry the version and release date.
+- Describe only what exists on the current branch; planned features go to the roadmap. Check every command,
+  option, path and configuration key against the code or the `--help` output before you write it down.
+- Write for newcomers first, then add the details. No marketing language.
+- Change both language versions in the same pull request.
+
+---
 
-Documentation First. If a feature isn't documented, it doesn't exist.
+## 10. Reporting security problems
 
-Gold Standard. We prefer "Stable & Secure" over "Fast & Broken".
+Please do not report vulnerabilities in a public issue. Open a private security advisory on GitHub instead.
 
-Happy Coding!
+Be respectful. We prefer stable and secure over fast and broken.
