-## 🛠️ Configuration
+## 🛡️ Security model
+
+- **Sandboxed builds:** conversion containers run as your user instead of root, without network access,
+  without Linux capabilities, with `no-new-privileges`, a read-only image filesystem and a process limit
+  (`build_pids_limit`, default 4096); a memory limit can be set (`build_memory_limit`). Only the build's own
+  folders (work space, output) are writable. Models are downloaded on the host beforehand.
+- **Tool permissions and audit log:** AI clients connected over MCP act only through registered tools with
+  risk categories. What runs on its own, what asks and what is denied is your decision. Every call lands in an
+  HMAC-chained audit log (`~/.llm-framework/audit.db`); `llm-cli tools audit --verify` detects changes.
+- **SSH trust on first use:** an unknown board's host key is shown with its SHA256 fingerprint and stored in the
+  framework's own `~/.llm-framework/known_hosts` only after you confirm. A changed key is always refused.
+- **No stored passwords:** the SSH password is used once to install the framework's Ed25519 key on the board.
+  API keys and tokens are encrypted; the key for them lives in your operating system's keyring.
+- **Untrusted text:** web pages, knowledge-base text, build logs and probe files are cleaned of hidden
+  characters and passed to the AI as clearly marked data, never as instructions. Chat output is escaped, and
+  links open only after you confirm.
+- **Crawler and archives:** the documentation crawler blocks private addresses (SSRF protection), respects
+  `robots.txt` and limits redirects and requests. Archive extraction blocks path traversal, links and device
+  files.
+
