+# Blueprint
+
+This is the design document of the LLM Cross-Compiler Framework. It explains what the framework is for,
+how its parts fit together, how data moves through it, how it protects you, and why it is built the way
+it is. It ends with the [roadmap](#roadmap). It describes version 3.0.0; the history of earlier versions is
+in the [changelog](../CHANGELOG.md).
+
+## Contents
+
+- [In short](#in-short)
+- [Purpose and principles](#purpose-and-principles)
+- [Architecture overview](#architecture-overview)
+- [Data flows](#data-flows)
+- [Security model](#security-model)
+- [Key decisions](#key-decisions)
+- [Versioning and documentation policy](#versioning-and-documentation-policy)
+- [Roadmap](#roadmap)
+- [History](#history)
+- [Glossary](#glossary)
+
+## In short
+
+A language model as published on Hugging Face is usually too big and in the wrong format for a small
+computer such as a Radxa ZERO 3W. The framework fixes that in three steps:
+
+1. **Fetch** the model (a Hugging Face repo id such as `Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B`, or a folder on your disk).
+2. **Convert and shrink** it ("quantize") inside a Docker container that has the right tools for your
+   hardware.
+3. **Install** the result on your device over SSH.
+
+You can drive it in three ways: the desktop GUI, the command line `llm-cli`, or an AI client such as Claude
+Code or Claude Desktop through the Model Context Protocol (MCP). All three use the same core.
+
+In 3.0.0 there is one official hardware target, **Rockchip** (boards RK3566, RK3568, RK3576, RK3588), with
+three output formats:
+
+| Format | Runs on | Boards | Typical use |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| GGUF (llama.cpp) | CPU | all four | chat models on any board |
+| RKLLM | NPU | RK3588, RK3576 | chat models on the neural processor |
+| RKNN | NPU | all four | ONNX vision and audio models |
+
+`llm-cli` is the console command defined in `pyproject.toml`. If it is not on your PATH, run
+`python -m orchestrator.cli` from the installation folder instead; the options are the same.
+
+## Purpose and principles
+
+The framework exists so that people without a background in compilers or machine learning can run a
+language model on their own small hardware, and so that experts get a reproducible, inspectable pipeline.
+Five principles guide every design choice.
+
+| Principle | What it means in practice |
+|---|---|
+| **Local-first** | Conversions run on your computer in Docker. Models are downloaded on the host and the conversion containers have no network. Apart from downloads, nothing leaves your computer unless you use a cloud AI provider for the assistant Ditto or share a knowledge pack yourself (see [Security model](#security-model) for what Ditto sends). |
+| **Laypeople-friendly** | The GUI offers only what the selected target supports, the CLI refuses anything else, and both fill in sensible defaults per board (for example Q4_0 on the RK3566). `llm-cli doctor` explains problems with fix hints. Self-healing proposals are written in plain words and wait for your decision. |
+| **Safe by default** | The conversion sandbox is on (`build_sandbox: true`). AI clients may read freely but must ask before changing anything. New SSH devices are trusted only after you confirm their fingerprint. Passwords are never stored. Text suggested by an AI is never executed. |
+| **Data over code** | What a target can build is declared in its `target.yml` (`capabilities:` block, checked by a schema). Calibration text is shipped as checksummed data. Tools are typed schemas. Adding a board or a quantization type to a target is an edit of data, as long as the target's build script supports it. |
+| **Verifiable builds** | Every build writes a manifest (`build_info.json`) with the SHA256 hash of each model file, a `Model_Card.md` and a ZIP archive. The deployment package checks the hashes on the device before installing. Dependencies are pinned by hash; the tool audit log can be verified. |
+
+## Architecture overview
+
+In plain words: there are three front doors (GUI, CLI, MCP). Behind them sits one core, the
+`FrameworkManager`, which starts all other parts and hands them to each other. A build request ends up in
+the `BuildEngine`, which builds a Docker image for the target and runs the conversion in sandboxed
+containers. The finished model lands in `output/`, and the `DeploymentManager` copies it to the device.
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TB
+    subgraph Doors["Front doors"]
+        GUI["GUI<br/>orchestrator/gui"]
+        CLI["CLI llm-cli<br/>orchestrator/cli.py"]
+        CLIENT["MCP client<br/>Claude Code, Claude Desktop, VS Code"]
+    end
+    MCPS["MCP server<br/>orchestrator/mcp_server.py"]
+
+    subgraph Core["FrameworkManager - orchestrator/Core/framework.py"]
+        CFG["ConfigManager"]
+        SEC["SecretsManager<br/>OS keyring"]
+        TM["TargetManager<br/>+ capabilities schema"]
+        TOOLS["Tool registry<br/>policy + audit log"]
+        BS["BuildService"]
+        ORC["LLMOrchestrator"]
+        HEAL["Self-healing"]
+        DITTO["Ditto<br/>LiteLLM-based in 3.0.0"]
+        RAG["RAG + crawler<br/>Qdrant"]
+        DOC["Doctor"]
+        DEP["DeploymentManager<br/>+ ssh_trust"]
+    end
+
+    BE["BuildEngine<br/>orchestrator/Core/builder.py"]
+    subgraph Docker["Docker with BuildKit"]
+        IMG["Target image<br/>targets/Rockchip/dockerfile"]
+        SBX["Sandboxed stage containers<br/>imatrix + build via modules/build.sh"]
+    end
+    TGT["targets/<br/>target.yml + modules"]
+    CAL["data/calibration"]
+    OUT["output/<br/>model files, build_info.json, ZIP"]
+    DEV["Edge device<br/>e.g. Radxa ZERO 3W"]
+
+    CLIENT -->|"stdio or local HTTP"| MCPS
+    MCPS --> TOOLS
+    TOOLS --> BS
+    TOOLS --> DOC
+    TOOLS --> RAG
+    BS --> ORC
+    GUI --> ORC
+    CLI --> ORC
+    GUI --> DEP
+    CLI --> DEP
+    CLI --> TOOLS
+    CLI --> DOC
+    GUI --> DITTO
+    BS --> TM
+    GUI --> TM
+    CLI --> TM
+    TM --> TGT
+    ORC --> CAL
+    ORC --> HEAL
+    HEAL --> DITTO
+    DITTO --> RAG
+    ORC --> BE
+    BE --> SEC
+    BE --> IMG
+    IMG --> SBX
+    SBX --> OUT
+    DEP --> OUT
+    DEP -->|"SSH, trust on first use"| DEV
+```
+
+### Components
+
+| Component | Where | Role |
+|---|---|---|
+| GUI | `orchestrator/gui/` (start: `llm-gui`) | Main window, build tab, deployment window, Ditto chat, module wizard, Hugging Face browser, dialogs for tool permissions, healing proposals and SSH trust. |
+| CLI | `orchestrator/cli.py` (`llm-cli`) | Command groups `build`, `targets`, `deploy`, `doctor`, `mcp`, `tools`, `ditto`, `module`, `secrets`, `swarm`, `scan`, `config`. |
+| FrameworkManager | `orchestrator/Core/framework.py` | Starts all managers in four phases (security and infrastructure, core logic, AI and knowledge, guardians and operations), connects them, keeps one long-lived asyncio loop for builds and resolves relative data paths against the installation folder, not the working directory. |
+| ConfigManager | `orchestrator/Core/config_manager.py` | Settings with a schema (type, default, validation rule). User settings go to `config.yml` in the config folder (usually `configs/`). Personal state lives in `~/.llm-framework` (override with `LLM_FRAMEWORK_HOME`), mode 0700 on Linux and macOS. |
+| SecretsManager | `orchestrator/Core/secrets_manager.py` | Encrypted store (`secrets.store` in the config folder, Fernet) whose master key is kept in the operating system's keyring. Holds API keys and the Hugging Face token (`hf_token`). |
+| TargetManager and capabilities | `orchestrator/Core/target_manager.py`, `orchestrator/schemas/targets.py` | Loads the `target.yml` of every target folder (folders starting with `_`, such as `_template`, are skipped) and validates its `capabilities:` block: boards, formats, quantization types (also per board), board defaults, importance matrix and calibration needs, support level (`official`, `community`, `experimental`). |
+| BuildService | `orchestrator/Core/build_service.py`, `orchestrator/schemas/build.py` | One typed entry point for builds: validate, start, status, cancel, log tail, artifacts, healing decision. The MCP tools use it. GUI and CLI check the same capabilities and then submit to the orchestrator directly. |
+| LLMOrchestrator | `orchestrator/Core/orchestrator.py` | Queue and pipeline: prepares calibration text, expands a request into jobs (model x target x format x quantization), runs them on the build engine, handles cancellation and the self-healing approval loop. |
+| BuildEngine | `orchestrator/Core/builder.py`, `orchestrator/utils/build_context.py` | Stages the Docker build context, builds the image (BuildKit when available), downloads or mounts the model, runs the optional Trivy scan, the importance-matrix stage and the build stage in sandboxed containers, writes the manifest, model card and ZIP, and cleans up. |
+| DockerManager | `orchestrator/Core/docker_manager.py` | Docker connection, live CPU/RAM figures for the GUI, the optional Qdrant container. |
+| Rockchip reference target | `targets/Rockchip/` | `dockerfile` (Debian bookworm-slim; llama.cpp v0.6.0 tools; three Python venvs), `modules/build.sh` (dispatcher for `gguf`, `rkllm`, `rknn` and the `imatrix` job), `modules/rkllm_module.sh`, `modules/rknn_module.sh`, `scripts/export_rkllm.py`, `scripts/rknn_converter.py`, `target.yml`. `targets/_template/` is the starting point for new targets. |
+| DeploymentManager and SSH trust | `orchestrator/Core/deployment_manager.py`, `orchestrator/utils/ssh_trust.py` | Builds the deployment package, checks the device's host key, pairs the framework's SSH key, uploads and runs the installer. |
+| Tool registry, policy, audit | `orchestrator/tools/` (`registry.py`, `policy.py`, `audit.py`, `framework_tools.py`) | Eleven typed tools with risk tiers, the user's stored permissions, confirmations and a hash-chained audit log. |
+| MCP server | `orchestrator/mcp_server.py` | Exposes the tool registry over MCP (official SDK `mcp` 2.3.0) via stdio or local Streamable HTTP. |
+| Ditto | `orchestrator/Core/ditto_manager.py` | The built-in assistant in its 3.0.0 form: LiteLLM 1.104.2 with the providers OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Ollama and Custom (any OpenAI-compatible server). It answers chat questions, analyses build logs for self-healing and drafts target modules from a hardware probe. An offline mode runs a small local model from `models/tiny_models` with Hugging Face Transformers (torch and transformers are not part of the default installation). |
+| Self-healing | `orchestrator/Core/self_healing_manager.py` | Recognizes known failures with fixed patterns first and asks Ditto only for unknown ones. Applies only framework-defined fixes, and only after your approval. |
+| RAG and crawler | `orchestrator/Core/rag_manager.py`, `orchestrator/Core/crawler_manager.py`, `orchestrator/Core/community_manager.py` | Optional knowledge base (`enable_rag_knowledge`): web pages and PDFs are crawled, split, embedded and stored in Qdrant. Knowledge packs can be shared as JSON in `community/knowledge/`. |
+| Doctor | `orchestrator/Core/doctor.py` | Deterministic health checks (Python, Docker and BuildKit, disk space, targets, state folder permissions, secrets, tool permissions and audit log, calibration data) with stable codes, fix hints and tiered automatic fixes. |
+| Calibration data | `data/calibration/`, `orchestrator/utils/calibration.py` | Curated German, English and tool-use text (MIT licence) with SHA256 checks, used for importance matrices. |
+| Untrusted text handling | `orchestrator/utils/untrusted.py` | Cleans and wraps external text before it reaches a language model. |
+
+The Rockchip folder also still contains `source_module.sh`, `config_module.sh`, `convert_module.sh` and
+`target_module.sh` from the four-module chain of version 1.x. The current dispatcher `build.sh` does not
+call them; their removal is planned for 3.4.0.
+
+`configs/project_sources.yml` is a catalogue of upstream projects and documentation links, checked by
+`scripts/validate_ssot.py`. The framework does not read it at runtime in 3.0.0; corrections are planned for
+3.4.0.
+
+### Where things are stored
+
+| Place | Content |
+|---|---|
+| `targets/` | Target definitions (one folder per target). |
+| `cache/models/hf/` | Models downloaded from Hugging Face. |
+| `cache/builds/<build id>/` | Work folder of a running build (build context, output, importance matrix, scratch space); removed after the build. |
+| `cache/calibration/` | Calibration text written for importance matrices. |
+| `output/` | Finished builds, one folder per build with model files, `build_info.json`, `Model_Card.md`, plus a ZIP next to it. |
+| `~/.llm-framework/` (or `LLM_FRAMEWORK_HOME`) | `tool_policy.json`, `audit.db` with `audit.db.anchor`, `known_hosts`, the framework SSH key `id_ed25519_llm_framework` (+ `.pub`), `mcp_http_token`, `chat_history.json`. |
+| Config folder (usually `configs/`) | `config.yml` (user settings) and `secrets.store` (encrypted). |
+
+## Data flows
+
+### A build, from request to device
+
+In plain words: you pick a model, a board and a quantization. The framework checks that the target can do
+it, prepares everything on your computer, builds a toolbox image, converts the model in a locked-down
+container, records hashes and lets you install the result on your device.
+
+1. **Request.** The GUI build tab, `llm-cli build start -m <model> -t Rockchip -f gguf -b rk3566`, or the
+   MCP tool `start_build` creates a request. It is checked against the target's capabilities: unknown
+   formats, boards or quantization types are refused with a sentence that lists the allowed values; a
+   missing quantization is filled with the board default; low-bit types such as IQ2_XS require an
+   importance matrix.
+2. **Queue and calibration.** The `LLMOrchestrator` queues the request. If an importance matrix is wanted
+   (GGUF only) and you gave no dataset, it writes the shipped calibration text
+   (`calibration_language`: `mixed`, `de` or `en`) to `cache/calibration/`.
+3. **Jobs.** The request is expanded into one job per model, target, format and quantization. Each job goes
+   to the `BuildEngine`.
+4. **Build context and image.** The engine copies the target's `modules/`, `scripts/` and the shared
+   `entrypoint.sh` into a fresh build context and builds the image `llm-framework/<target>:<build id>`
+   with `docker buildx build` (BuildKit), or with the Docker API if BuildKit is missing. This step needs
+   network: it downloads llama.cpp (tag from `llama_cpp_version`, default `v0.6.0`) and the Python
+   toolchains. The number of parallel compile jobs comes from `build_jobs` (0 means all cores).
+5. **Staging on the host.** The model is made available before any conversion code runs: a local folder or
+   file is mounted read-only; a Hugging Face repo is downloaded on the host into `cache/models/hf/`, using
+   your stored `hf_token` if needed. The token never enters a container.
+6. **Security scan.** If `enable_security_scan` is on (default), Trivy (`image_trivy`, default
+   `aquasec/trivy:0.75.0`) scans the image for HIGH and CRITICAL vulnerabilities. Findings produce a warning;
+   the build continues.
+7. **Importance-matrix stage** (GGUF with IMatrix only). A sandboxed container runs `modules/build.sh` with
+   `JOB_TYPE=imatrix`: it converts the model to F16 GGUF and runs `llama-imatrix` on the calibration text
+   (first 100 chunks by default, `IMATRIX_CHUNKS`). If this stage fails, the build stops.
+8. **Build stage.** A second sandboxed container runs `modules/build.sh` with `JOB_TYPE=build`. The
+   dispatcher picks the toolchain: `llama-quantize` for GGUF (with the importance matrix if present),
+   `rkllm_module.sh` for RKLLM (RK3588 and RK3576 only), `rknn_module.sh` for RKNN.
+9. **Manifest and golden artifact.** The engine copies the results to `output/`, completes
+   `build_info.json` with every model file's size and SHA256 hash and the primary file, writes
+   `Model_Card.md` and creates a ZIP of the build folder. The per-build image tag and the work folder are
+   removed; image layers stay in the build cache, so the next build is faster.
+10. **Deployment.** In the deployment window (or with `llm-cli deploy package` and `llm-cli deploy run`)
+    you pick a build. The framework packs the model file, `build_info.json`, `Model_Card.md`, a generated
+    `deploy.sh` and `checksums.sha256` into a ZIP.
+11. **Trust on first use.** Before the first connection the device's host key fingerprint is shown
+    (the same format as `ssh-keygen -lf`). Only after you confirm it, or pass a matching
+    `--trust-fingerprint`, is it stored in the framework's own `known_hosts`. A changed key always stops
+    the deployment.
+12. **Pairing and install.** On the first connection your password is used once to install the framework's
+    Ed25519 key on the device; it is never stored. The package is uploaded into a private temporary folder
+    (`mktemp -d /tmp/llm_deploy.XXXXXXXX`), unpacked, and `deploy.sh` checks all hashes with
+    `sha256sum -c` before it copies the model to `$MODEL_DIR` (default `$HOME/models`) and prints how to
+    start it (for GGUF with `llama-server`).
+
+The MCP tool `cancel_build` stops the running stage container. Each stage container has its own time
+limit (`build_timeout`, default 3600 seconds).
+
+### A tool call from an MCP client
+
+In plain words: the AI client may look at things on its own, but before it changes something the framework
+asks you, through the client, and writes down what happened.
+
+```mermaid
+sequenceDiagram
+    participant U as You
+    participant C as MCP client
+    participant S as MCP server
+    participant R as Tool registry
+    participant B as BuildService
+    C->>S: call start_build (model, target, format, board)
+    S->>S: check arguments against the input schema
+    S->>R: decide: allow, ask or deny
+    alt your permission says "ask"
+        S->>C: elicitation "Allow this action?"
+        C->>U: shows the question
+        U-->>C: yes or no
+        C-->>S: approve true or false
+    end
+    S->>R: call start_build with the answer
+    R->>R: validate input, enforce permission
+    R->>B: start_build(request)
+    B-->>R: request id
+    R->>R: write audit entry
+    R-->>S: typed result
+    S-->>C: structured result
+```
+
+1. **Schema.** Every tool has a pydantic input and output model. The MCP SDK publishes them as JSON
+   schemas; invalid arguments are refused before anything runs.
+2. **Policy.** The registry looks up your stored permission for this tool (per-tool override first, then
+   its tier). Defaults: `read` allowed, `local_write` ask, `external` ask. Tools in the `forbidden` tier
+   cannot even be registered.
+3. **Elicitation.** "Ask" becomes an MCP elicitation: the client shows you the tool name and its arguments.
+   A client without elicitation support cannot confirm, so the call is denied with a hint to allow the tool
+   with `llm-cli tools permissions` or in the GUI (Tools > Tool Permissions (Ditto / MCP)...).
+4. **Execution.** The handler runs in a worker thread; its result is checked against the output model.
+   Errors come back as tool errors with a message meant for people.
+5. **Audit.** Every outcome (`ok`, `denied`, `not_confirmed`, `error`, `invalid_input`) is appended to
+   `audit.db` with redacted, shortened arguments and a hash of the full text. `llm-cli tools audit --verify`
+   checks the chain and its anchor.
+
+The eleven tools in 3.0.0: `list_targets`, `get_target_capabilities`, `list_build_requests`,
+`get_build_status`, `read_build_log`, `list_artifacts`, `run_doctor`, `search_knowledge` (tier `read`) and
+`start_build`, `cancel_build`, `resolve_healing` (tier `local_write`). No tool of the `external` tier is
+registered, so AI clients cannot deploy to devices.
+
+### Self-healing approval
+
+In plain words: when a build fails, the framework looks for a known cause, tells you what it would do, and
+waits. Nothing is changed without your yes.
+
+1. A job fails. The orchestrator hands the error log to the self-healing manager.
+2. **Known issues first.** Fixed patterns recognize, for example, an out-of-memory compiler during the image
+   build, a killed conversion (exit code 137), a full disk, missing Docker permissions, DNS failures and
+   outdated apt sources.
+3. **Ditto second.** Only if no pattern matches, Ditto analyses the log (wrapped as untrusted text). Its
+   answer is always treated as advice: the strategy is set to "manual step".
+4. **Waiting for you.** The build status becomes `awaiting_approval`. You decide in the GUI dialog, at the
+   prompt of a running `llm-cli build start`, or through the MCP tool `resolve_healing`. Without a
+   decision within `healing_approval_timeout` (default 900 seconds) the proposal is dismissed.
+5. **Only fixed actions.** An approved proposal is applied only if it is one of three framework-defined
+   strategies: lower `build_jobs` to 1, remove dangling framework images and the Docker build cache, or
+   simply retry. The job is then retried once. Command text from patterns or AI is shown read-only and
+   never executed; approving a manual step through MCP is refused.
+
+## Security model
+
+In plain words: the framework works with third-party conversion tools, AI-generated code and text from the
+internet. It treats all of them as potentially hostile and limits what each can reach. The table lists
+each threat, the protection in 3.0.0 and where it is implemented.
+
+| Threat | Protection in 3.0.0 | Where |
+|---|---|---|
+| Conversion code (vendor toolkits, AI-generated target scripts) harms the host or sends data out | Conversion stages run as the host user instead of root (1000:1000 if the host user is root or on Windows), with no network (`network_mode: none`), all capabilities dropped, `no-new-privileges`, a read-only image filesystem, a 1 GB `/tmp` tmpfs, a process limit (`build_pids_limit`, default 4096) and an optional memory limit (`build_memory_limit`). Only the per-build folders (work space, output, importance matrix) are writable; model, cache and dataset mounts are read-only. Hugging Face libraries are forced offline. `build_sandbox: false` switches back to the old root-plus-entrypoint mode. | `BuildEngine._sandbox` in `orchestrator/Core/builder.py` |
+| The crawler is abused to reach internal services (SSRF), also through DNS rebinding or redirects | Only http(s); URLs with backslashes, whitespace, control characters or ambiguous hosts are refused; every resolved address must be public (no private, loopback, link-local, CGNAT, multicast or reserved ranges, also inside IPv4-mapped, NAT64, 6to4 and Teredo addresses). At connect time the address is resolved and checked again and the connection goes to exactly that IP. Redirects are followed manually (at most 5, each checked); a request budget of five times `crawler_max_pages`; robots.txt per RFC 9309 (server errors mean "disallow"); delay between requests (`crawler_rate_limit`). Behind a configured proxy only the URL check applies. | `validate_public_url`, `is_public_ip` in `orchestrator/utils/validation.py`; `PublicOnlyAdapter` in `orchestrator/Core/crawler_manager.py` |
+| Instructions hidden in external text steer a language model (prompt injection) | Web pages, knowledge-base hits, build logs, hardware probes and conversation summaries never go into the system prompt. They are normalized, stripped of invisible characters and HTML comments, shortened and wrapped in a block with a random id; the system prompt tells the model to treat such blocks as data. MCP tool descriptions and server instructions mark logs and search results as untrusted. Chat output is HTML-escaped; links open only after confirmation. Ditto's errors and refusals raise errors instead of appearing as answers. | `orchestrator/utils/untrusted.py`, `orchestrator/Core/ditto_manager.py`, `orchestrator/gui/chat_window.py`, `orchestrator/mcp_server.py` |
+| A generated or shared target module contains harmful code | Before any template is rendered, names, architecture, Debian package names, compiler/CMake flags and the detection command are checked against strict patterns; base images must be pinned by digest unless they are official base images (`debian:`, `ubuntu:`, `nvidia/cuda:`, `rocm/dev-ubuntu-*`, `rocm/rocm-terminal:`, `python:`). Shell code fields must pass a fixed deny-list (downloads, `sudo`, deleting `/`, `chmod 777`, raw device writes, Docker socket or `docker run`, evaluating generated code) and every generated script must pass `bash -n`. New targets stay inside `targets/`, never overwrite an existing target and are removed again if generation fails. The wizard shows the shell code for review. | `validate_module_data`, `ModuleGenerator` in `orchestrator/Core/module_generator.py` |
+| An AI client or Ditto does something you did not want | Four risk tiers; the registry enforces your stored permissions on every call, whatever the client's tool annotations say. `forbidden` tools cannot be registered; `external` tools can only be allowed one by one, never as a whole tier; a damaged permission file falls back to the safe defaults. Confirmations go through MCP elicitation. Every call is recorded in an audit log whose entries are chained with an HMAC; the key is a separate entry in the OS keyring, not next to the database (without a keyring the chain is unkeyed, and the check says so). An anchor file (`audit.db.anchor`) also reveals removed newest entries. | `orchestrator/tools/` |
+| Other local programs or websites talk to the MCP server | stdio is the default. Streamable HTTP binds only to loopback addresses, requires a per-user bearer token (compared in constant time, file `mcp_http_token`, mode 0600) and uses the SDK's Host/Origin checks against DNS rebinding. | `orchestrator/mcp_server.py` |
+| Secrets leak | Secrets are stored encrypted with the master key in the OS keyring. API keys are passed per call, never exported to the environment of child processes. A custom endpoint URL is used only for the Ollama and Custom providers, so a cloud key never reaches a leftover local URL, and a Custom endpoint without a key gets a placeholder instead of `OPENAI_API_KEY`. The Hugging Face token stays on the host. The SSH password is used once and never stored. Known key formats (OpenAI/Anthropic `sk-`, Hugging Face `hf_`, Google `AIza`, GitHub tokens) are redacted in build logs and errors handed to tools, in the audit log, telemetry reports and community exports. | `orchestrator/Core/secrets_manager.py`, `orchestrator/Core/ditto_manager.py`, `redact_secrets` in `orchestrator/utils/helpers.py` |
+| Someone impersonates your device during deployment | Trust on first use, but never silently: unknown host keys are shown with their SHA256 fingerprint and stored only after confirmation; a changed key or key type always fails. Authentication uses the framework's own Ed25519 key. The OpenSSH fallback runs with `BatchMode=yes` and `StrictHostKeyChecking=yes`. User names and IP addresses are validated; the upload goes into a private temporary folder; `deploy.sh` verifies all checksums. Without Docker only model files are deployed, never arbitrary programs. | `orchestrator/utils/ssh_trust.py`, `orchestrator/Core/deployment_manager.py` |
+| A crafted archive writes outside its folder | Extraction refuses path traversal, links pointing outside and device files, and uses Python's `data` filter for tar files. | `safe_extract_archive` in `orchestrator/utils/helpers.py` |
+| A compromised dependency (supply chain) | Host dependencies are pinned with hashes (`requirements*.txt`, Poetry 2 lock); `litellm` (1.104.2) and `mcp` (2.3.0) are pinned exactly. GitHub Actions are pinned by commit SHA; CI runs pip-audit and bandit; Dependabot checks pip, Actions and Docker weekly. In the Rockchip image the RKLLM wheel is checked by SHA256 and the toolkit, torch and onnx versions are pinned. The calibration text is checked by SHA256. | `pyproject.toml`, `poetry.lock`, `.github/`, `targets/Rockchip/dockerfile`, `orchestrator/utils/calibration.py` |
+
+### Known gaps in 3.0.0
+
+These are real limitations of the current version. Most are scheduled in the [roadmap](#roadmap).
+
+- **What Ditto sends to the cloud.** With a cloud provider, Ditto's chat messages, build-log excerpts for
+  self-healing and hardware probes for module generation go to that provider. The knowledge base computes
+  embeddings with OpenAI (`text-embedding-3-small`), so ingested text and search queries go to OpenAI as
+  well. The setting `ai_security_level` exists in the configuration schema but has no effect yet; the
+  privacy filter and local embeddings are planned for 3.1.0. Offline mode disables cloud calls and the
+  knowledge base.
+- **Qdrant port.** The optional Qdrant container publishes port 6333 with Docker's default binding, which
+  is all network interfaces of the host, not only loopback. The embedded Qdrant planned for 3.1.0 removes
+  the container.
+- **Community knowledge packs** are only obfuscated (XOR "SwarmCipher"), not signed. Signed packs with
+  review and quarantine are planned for 3.4.0.
+- **Trivy and the Docker socket.** The scanner container gets the Docker socket; mounting it read-only does
+  not limit what the Docker API allows. Planned for 3.4.0: Trivy pinned by digest, without the socket.
+- **Pins inside images.** Base images are pinned by tag (`debian:bookworm-slim`), llama.cpp by tag
+  (`v0.6.0`), and the venvs inside the image have no hashed lock files. Planned for 3.4.0.
+- **Image builds have network access.** This is by design (they download toolchains); only the conversion
+  stages are offline.
+
+## Key decisions
+
+| Decision | Why |
+|---|---|
+| **One venv per toolchain** in the target image | The toolchains pin conflicting versions: the llama.cpp converter uses a current torch (2.11, CPU), RKLLM-Toolkit 1.3.1 needs torch 2.6 and its own transformers, RKNN-Toolkit2 2.3.2 needs torch 2.4, setuptools below 81 and onnx 1.16.2. Separate venvs (`/opt/venv`, `/opt/venv-rkllm`, `/opt/venv-rknn`) keep each at its tested versions; the module scripts call the right interpreter. |
+| **Debian bookworm and Python 3.11 in the images** | Rockchip publishes its toolkit wheels only for CPython 3.10 to 3.12 (the image uses the cp311 RKLLM wheel); bookworm ships Python 3.11. The host side is independent and supports Python 3.11 to 3.14. |
+| **BuildKit for image builds** | Better layer caching than the legacy builder behind docker-py's `api.build`, which Docker has deprecated, and the same builder CI uses. The engine falls back to the Docker API if `docker buildx` is missing. |
+| **Capabilities as data** | One declaration in `target.yml` feeds the GUI, the CLI, the build service and the MCP tools, so they cannot disagree. The pydantic schema rejects inconsistent declarations (for example a default that is not in the list); tests in `tests/test_foundation.py` keep it in sync with `build.sh` and `export_rkllm.py`. |
+| **Stage models on the host, run the sandbox without network** | Downloads need network and sometimes a token. Doing them on the host lets every piece of conversion code run offline: vendor toolkits and AI-generated target scripts cannot reach the internet or your LAN, and the token never enters a container. |
+| **MCP first** | Many users already have a capable AI client (Claude Code, Claude Desktop, VS Code). Exposing typed tools with permissions and an audit log made the framework usable from those clients before the built-in assistant is rebuilt, and the client's credentials stay in the client. The same registry is meant for Ditto's coming agent loop. |
+| **Official SDKs instead of LiteLLM for the coming provider layer** (3.1.0, in progress) | LiteLLM brings a large dependency tree (among others boto3, aiohttp, tokenizers, jinja2 and python-dotenv) and was itself hit by a PyPI supply-chain compromise (versions 1.82.7 and 1.82.8, March 2026); that is why 3.0.0 pins it exactly. The official `anthropic` and `openai` SDKs cover the planned providers directly or through OpenAI-compatible APIs, with fewer layers in between. |
+| **No Claude subscription login (OAuth) inside the framework** | Anthropic does not allow third-party apps to use Claude subscription logins. There are two supported ways instead: use a Claude subscription in Anthropic's own MCP clients (Claude Code, Claude Desktop) that drive this framework, or give Ditto an Anthropic API key. 3.1.0 plans the user's own, unmodified Claude Code CLI as an optional provider. |
+| **Curated calibration data shipped instead of generated** | An importance matrix is only as good as the text it is computed on. The old generator downloaded a URL that returned 404 and fell back to repeated sentences. 3.0.0 ships subsets of `eaddario/imatrix-calibration` (MIT, pinned revision), checks their SHA256 and interleaves them so that the first chunks `llama-imatrix` reads already cover German, English and tool use. |
+| **Only fixed self-healing actions** | A build log can contain text written by anyone, and model output can be wrong. Letting either one run commands on the host would be a remote-code-execution path. AI output is therefore advice; the framework applies only three fixed strategies, and only with your approval. |
+| **Trust on first use with confirmation, framework key instead of passwords** | Silently accepting host keys makes man-in-the-middle attacks easy; stored passwords leak. Confirming a fingerprint once and pairing a dedicated key gives password-free, verifiable deployments. |
+| **One version source** | `orchestrator.__version__` is the only place the version is written in code; a test keeps `pyproject.toml` and the changelog in line, so the GUI, CLI and MCP server always report the same version. |
+
+## Versioning and documentation policy
+
+- **Semantic Versioning 2.0.0.** MAJOR for incompatible changes, MINOR for new features, PATCH for fixes.
+- **Keep a Changelog 1.1.0.** [CHANGELOG.md](../CHANGELOG.md) (English) and
+  [CHANGELOG_DE.md](../CHANGELOG_DE.md) (German) record what each released version contains, with an
+  `[Unreleased]` section at the top. Planned work is listed only in the [roadmap](#roadmap) below.
+- **Single version source.** `orchestrator/__init__.py` holds `__version__`; `pyproject.toml` carries the
+  same value. `tests/test_versioning.py` checks that `pyproject.toml`, the newest changelog entry, the
+  framework info and the CLI agree. `llm-cli --version` prints it.
+- **English first, German second.** Every document exists twice: `<name>.md` in English and
+  `<name>_DE.md` in German, with the same structure and content and a link to its counterpart at the top.
+  The German text is written as natural German, not as a word-by-word translation.
+- **Version line.** Every document starts with its version and date ("Version 3.0.0 · Last updated
+  2026-10-10", in German "Version 3.0.0 · Stand 2026-10-10"). README and changelog carry versions and
+  release dates.
+- **Only what exists.** Documents describe the current branch. Work in progress appears only in the
+  roadmap.
